# The elastic build tier + the compile cache

How a nexus runs **heavy build work off its serving path** — compiling packages to
wasm, running per-tenant CI/CD — on ephemeral Fly Firecracker workers, and how a
**content-addressed compile cache** turns "compile on every deploy" into "compile once,
fetch forever." This is the design; it is not built yet. Tracking: see the build-tier epic.

## The gap this fills

A nexus already runs wasm sandboxes — but it also has to *produce* them. Today the
[compile lanes](/capabilities/compile-lanes) (rust/c/zig/js → wasm component) run
**inside the serving nexus**: a single 450–900 MB wasmtime compiler process, ~7–20 s,
shelled on the same box that serves pages and runs agents. On a 1 GB Starter machine
that compile competes with everything else for RAM, and a cold package is recompiled
from scratch every time anyone needs it. Two problems — *placement* (heavy work on the
serving box) and *waste* (recompiling identical inputs) — and both have the same fix:
move the work out, and never do it twice.

## The fourth compute mode: a `job`

The runtime already has three compute modes. The build tier adds a fourth.

| Mode | What it is | Where it runs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `server` | native BEAM units | the serving nexus |
| `sandbox` | in-nexus wasm with capability grants | the serving nexus process |
| `agent` | an ephemeral *LLM* sandboxed function | dispatched, short-lived |
| **`job`** *(new)* | an ephemeral *build-class* unit — compile, test, pipeline step | **a Firecracker worker, off the serving path** |

A `job` is one isolated unit of heavy work that runs on an ephemeral Fly Firecracker
microVM the nexus dispatches, returns an artifact + logs, and disappears. It is
**per-tenant**, **billed per-second**, and **scales to zero**. It is distinct from
`sandbox` precisely because `sandbox` must stay light and live — a `job` is allowed to
be heavy and slow because it never touches a request.

You do not hand-write a `job` block in the common case — the compile lane emits one
automatically when a compile is heavy or a cache miss needs filling. You *do* write
jobs explicitly for CI/CD pipelines (below).

## Two workloads, metered separately

The tier carries two workloads whose costs differ by ~600×. They must never share one
"build-minutes" bucket.

- **(a) Compile offload** — short, bursty. Shape: `shared-cpu-2x` / 2 GB, ~15 s active.
  **~$0.0001 per compile** (compute-seconds dominate once the image isn't re-pulled).
- **(b) In-nexus CI/CD** — longer pipelines (build → test → publish). Shape:
  `performance-4x` / 8 GB, ~10 min. **~$0.05 per run** (compute + modest egress).

This is *GitHub Actions, but native to the nexus and per-tenant*: the workflow lives in
`.work`, runs on your fleet, and publishes wasm components / images back into the nexus.
Per-second Fly billing beats GitHub Actions' per-minute rounding badly for short
compiles (a 15 s compile on GHA bills a 1-minute minimum, 60× our cost), and a 10-min
CI run on `performance-4x` is both cheaper *and* beefier than a GHA 2-core runner.

## The orchestration already exists

The trigger layer is the [reactive + time layer](/capabilities/hooks-and-time) we
already shipped. Nothing new is needed to *fire* a build:

- a `#event` on the bus, a `git push` (the [nexus is a git remote](/serving/git-remote)),
  or a `hook`'s `trigger` (`cron`/`every`/org-timestamp) →
- runs a `flow` whose steps are `job`s on workers →
- which publish artifacts (wasm components, images) the serving nexus consumes.

So "rebuild my wasm library on every push to this subtree" or "recompile nightly" are
just a `hook` + a `flow` over `job`s — no new authoring surface.

## The compile cache — "compile once per nexus, then fetch forever"

This is the lever that makes the whole tier cheap. **wasm compilation is
deterministic**: the same source + the same toolchain version + the same flags always
produce the same artifact. So it is naturally **content-addressable** — and a
content-addressed artifact is *tamper-evident by construction* (the key IS the hash of
the bytes; a corrupted or substituted artifact fails its own hash). This is the Nix
binary-cache / Bazel remote-cache idea, applied to wasm.

### The key

```
key = blake3( normalized_source ⊕ lane ⊕ toolchain_version ⊕ flags )
```

A compile request computes the key first. **Hit** → fetch the prebuilt `.wasm` from
storage (~$0, just egress, no worker spun). **Miss** → dispatch a compile `job`, store
the result under the key, then serve it. Most "compiles" become a fetch.

### Two scopes — the security boundary

The cache has two distinct namespaces, and the boundary between them is the whole
security model:

- **Shared package cache (public, global).** Public dependencies — an npm package, a
  crate, a Go module compiled to wasm — are the *same artifact for everyone* on the same
  version. The **first** tenant who needs `some-lib@3.1 → wasm` pays the one compile;
  **every other nexus, forever, gets a cache hit.** This is a wasm equivalent of a
  package registry's prebuilt-binary cache. Safe to share *because* it's
  content-addressed: you can only ever read back the exact bytes whose hash you asked
  for. Artifacts are signed for provenance on top of the hash.
- **Private tenant cache (per-nexus).** A tenant's *own* source compiles to artifacts
  that are theirs alone. These live in a tenant-scoped namespace, never the shared one,
  encrypted at rest, reachable only by that tenant's nexus. A tenant's input hash can
  *never* promote an artifact into the public cache — the scope is decided by the
  *source's* origin (declared public dependency vs. tenant subtree), not by the key.

### "Cold wasm storage" = the backing store

The cache uses **two distinct storage homes**, and the doc is explicit about which is
which because they are different products with different shapes:

- **Tigris** — the artifact store ("cold wasm storage"). Tigris is a **separate company**
  (Tigris Data) offering globally-distributed, S3-compatible **object** storage, available
  **through Fly as a storage partner** (`fly storage create`) — it is *not* Fly's own
  service. We use it because it fits a *global* shared cache: low-latency access from Fly
  machines, and objects auto-replicate near where they're pulled, so a package compiled
  once is cached close to every nexus that later requests it. The content-addressed
  artifacts (both scopes) live here as S3 objects, keyed by their hash.
- **Fly Volumes** — Fly's *own* persistent **block** storage, a volume attached to a
  single machine in a single region. This is where **per-machine local state** lives: the
  serving nexus's tenant SQLite / `WB_DATA` mount (the same store used for
  [Litestream and tenant DBs](/serving/tenant-db)), and a build worker's **baked compiler
  rootfs**. Volumes are region-pinned and machine-local — the right home for hot, local,
  per-instance state, and the wrong home for a global artifact cache (hence Tigris for
  that).

A small hot index (key → object) lets a hit skip the Tigris round-trip entirely. A small hot index (key → object) lets a hit skip the store round-trip entirely.
**No JSON sidecar** — the index is nexus state, the cache config is a `.work` `deploy`
block read via `Nexus.Config`.

### What the cache does to the economics

The per-compile cost in the pricing model is the cost of a **miss**. With a healthy
shared-cache hit rate, the *fleet* only ever compiles the long tail of genuinely-new
inputs; the common packages are compiled once across the entire customer base and
fetched thereafter. The compile tier's real cost tracks *cache misses*, not *compile
requests* — which is what makes including a generous compile allowance in even the free
tier viable.

## Security & isolation — the cache must not leak or be poisoned

Two attacks define the whole design: **confidentiality** (does a shared cache leak one
tenant's private code to another?) and **integrity** (can an attacker poison the cache
so a victim runs malicious bytes?). Both have clean answers, learned partly from how
other build caches failed.

### Confidentiality — you can only fetch what you can already name

A content-addressed artifact is addressed by `hash(content)`. To *request* an artifact
you must present its key, and to know the key you must know the content. **So you can
only ever retrieve an artifact whose source you already possess** — the cache hands back
nothing you didn't already hold. A tenant requesting a public package addresses it by
the **public registry's published integrity hash** (from their own lockfile); they
literally cannot *name* a private artifact whose source they don't have.

We do not rely on that argument alone. A **provenance gate at write time** decides the
namespace:

- **Shared/public** admission requires the source to have been fetched from a **public
  registry** *and* its integrity hash to **match what that registry published**. Nothing
  else qualifies.
- **Everything else** — a scoped/private package, a private registry, a git dependency,
  the tenant's own subtree source — goes to the **tenant-private** namespace only:
  encrypted at rest, ACL'd to that one tenant, and **never cross-tenant deduplicated**
  even when bytes match (so there is no "is anyone else compiling this proprietary lib?"
  existence oracle). The shared pool therefore only ever contains
  already-public-registry content.

Crucially, **nothing is ever keyed or admitted by package *name***. A malicious public
`@acme/utils@1.0` cannot shadow a private one of the same name — the requester addresses
by *their lockfile's integrity hash*, and admission verifies that hash against the
registry the source actually came from. That closes the **dependency-confusion** class
behind recent npm supply-chain attacks.

### Integrity — only trusted builders write the shared cache

The instructive failure is **CREEP** (CVE-2025-36852, severity 9.4): bucket-backed build
caches (S3/GCS/R2) poisoned by a *first-to-cache-wins* race — an attacker produces
malicious *output* from the same *input* and uploads it first. The lesson is sharp:
**input-based cache keys cannot prevent this**, because the poisoning happens at build
time, before any hashing — "correct and poisoned artifacts are identical from a
validation perspective." Nix's binary-cache history says the same: a content hash alone
is not a trust anchor — artifacts must be **signed**.

So our write path is gated, not open:

- **Tenants never write the shared cache.** They *trigger* a compile; **our** trusted
  Firecracker worker, running our **pinned, version-stamped toolchain**, performs the
  build and **signs** the artifact with the fleet key. There is no tenant-supplied-output
  upload path into the shared pool — so "first-to-cache-wins" is impossible; an attacker
  cannot write at all.
- **Readers verify the signature**, not just the hash. An unsigned or wrong-signed
  artifact is rejected and recompiled.
- **Untrusted CI contexts are scoped.** A PR / draft / preview build writes only to an
  **isolated, context-scoped** cache, never the shared production one — CREEP's
  "hierarchical write-access" mitigation applied to in-nexus CI/CD: protected refs write
  the shared cache, untrusted refs write their own branch-scoped cache.
- **Determinism audit (defense in depth).** Because the toolchain is pinned and wasm
  compiles are deterministic, a background rebuilder re-compiles a sample of shared
  artifacts on an independent worker and compares hashes; a mismatch raises a poisoning /
  non-determinism alarm (the reproducible-builds rebuilder pattern).

## Verifiable provenance — signatures over a transparency log (Radicle-pattern)

The integrity model above trusts *our* fleet signing key. We can do better and make every
artifact and every build **cryptographically verifiable by anyone**, with no central
authority to trust — the [Radicle](https://radicle.dev/) philosophy (self-certifying,
every action signed, trust from cryptography not from where data lives). Radicle itself
is **not** a blockchain, and neither is this — the same guarantees come from signed
records over a hash-linked log, at a fraction of the cost. Three layers:

- **Signed build attestations (artifact layer).** Every shared-cache artifact carries a
  signed statement — `{source_integrity_hash, lane, toolchain_version+digest,
  builder_pubkey, output_hash}` — signed by the builder key. This is the SLSA-provenance /
  in-toto attestation shape: *Radicle for build outputs.* A reader verifies the signature
  chains to a trusted builder root before accepting the artifact. **This is what actually
  defeats CREEP** — a poisoned artifact has no valid attestation from a trusted builder,
  regardless of matching input hashes.
- **A transparency log, not a blockchain (the record).** An append-only, hash-linked
  (Merkle) log of every attestation: tamper-evident, publicly/tenant-verifiable,
  history-immutable — the guarantees people mean by "on chain," without a chain
  (Certificate-Transparency / Sigstore-Rekor, and effectively how Radicle's signed
  history works). **This log is the same structure as the billing/provenance ledger
  below** — one append-only signed record serves both audit and metering, making billing
  non-repudiable.
- **Capability tokens for invocation (identity).** Each build runs under a signed, scoped
  capability token (biscuit/macaroon-style, extending the existing `wbk_` PAT /
  `Nexus.Auth`); the attestation records *who* invoked it, binding cost to a key rather
  than an IP. You cannot repudiate a build you signed for.

**The store is untrusted by design.** Trust lives in signatures + content hashes, so R2's
location or operator is irrelevant — a swapped or tampered artifact fails verification no
matter who controls the bucket. The nexus accepts only artifacts whose attestation is
signed by a builder key in its own trust root, so the store can inject nothing a builder
did not authorize. (Distinct surface, same philosophy: the *repo/git* side — the nexus is
already a git remote — is where the Radicle **protocol** itself fits, via self-certifying
signed repos; an interop target as native auth matures.)

## Usage, billing & abuse — airtight by construction

Billing must be admission-controlled, not just metered after the fact, or a tenant can
run unbilled or runaway compute.

- **Admission control before dispatch.** A compile/CI request checks the tenant's
  remaining allowance (`Nexus.Config` quota) **before** a worker is spawned. At zero:
  queue, degrade to the slow in-process path, or reject — **never** a silent unbilled
  run.
- **Hard per-job caps.** Firecracker enforces a wallclock, memory and CPU ceiling per
  job; a runaway is killed. A single compile can't exceed its bounded shape.
- **Per-tenant concurrency cap.** At most N concurrent workers per tenant — anti
  fork-bomb.
- **Single-flight dedup.** 100 concurrent requests for the same cache-miss key spawn
  **one** worker, not 100 — both a cost control and an abuse control.
- **Submission rate limit + egress cap** (the CI artifact-egress line).
- **The cache is the primary abuse mitigation.** Hits are ~free; only *misses* cost; and
  misses are capped + single-flighted. The one pathological case — a tenant forcing
  endless *unique* misses (randomized source) — is bounded by the quota backstop and a
  per-tenant miss-rate alarm.
- **Reconciled, idempotent ledger.** Metering is nexus state, reconciled against Fly's
  actual machine-seconds, so we bill *reality* and never drift over or under. No JSON
  sidecar — the ledger is runtime state, the quotas are `.work` `deploy` knobs.

## The fleet — cheapest viable shape

- **Baked compiler machine image, not per-job pull.** The compiler toolchain is ~7.2 GB.
  Pulling it per job dwarfs a 15 s compile (~10×) — fatal. Instead bake it into the
  worker's rootfs once; a **stopped** worker holds it for **$0.15/GB/mo (~$1.08/mo)** and
  **resumes in ~1–5 s** with no pull.
- **Scale to zero, resume on demand.** `auto_stop_machines` + `min_machines_running: 0`
  (already how `Nexus.Provisioner` builds machine config) — a worker costs only its
  stopped rootfs when idle.
- **Right shape per workload.** `shared-cpu-2x` for compiles, `performance-4x` for CI.
  Never one shared size.
- **Pin CI artifact output to R2**, never push large image layers back out through Fly
  egress ($0.02/GB) — egress can rival compute for image-publishing pipelines.
- **Reuse the existing seam.** Workers are provisioned through the same
  `Nexus.Provisioner` / `Nexus.Fly` machine-create path that already provisions tenant
  nexuses — not a second control plane.

## Tier mapping

Meter two units — `compile-jobs` and `ci-minutes` — as `.work` `deploy`-block knobs read
via `Nexus.Config`, neutral defaults in the runtime, our real numbers in our DeployKit
config (the same THE-LINE discipline as `tiers`). Indicative allowances against our
current tiers (Starter $0 / Team $49 / Scale $199):

| Tier | Included compiles/mo | Included CI | Cost to us | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Starter | ~3,000 | — | ~$1.38 | loss-leader; it's the OOM-relief that makes a 1 GB box usable |
| Team | ~60,000 | ~300 min | ~$7.50 | ~$41 gross headroom |
| Scale | ~600,000 | ~3,000 min | ~$75 | ~$124 gross headroom |

Overage (keep ~3–4× margin): compiles **~$1 / 5,000**, CI **~$0.015/min** (still under
GHA on stronger hardware). These numbers *assume cache misses are the minority* — the
shared cache is what keeps cost-to-us this low.

## The one cost risk

**Idle rootfs × tenant fan-out.** $1.08/mo per stopped baked worker is trivial per
tenant but **linear in tenant count** — 1,000 tenants each holding a 7.2 GB worker is
~$1,080/mo of pure idle storage. Mitigations, in order: (1) **share one compile-worker
app per N tenants** with per-tenant sandboxing rather than one image each; (2)
**garbage-collect** workers idle > X days, eat one cold re-bake on the next compile; (3)
**thin the baked image per-language** — pull only the toolchains a tenant's declared
`languages=` needs. Watch this line as tenant count grows; it's the only cost that
scales without usage. The compile cache makes (1) easy: a shared worker pool is fine
*because* the cache, not the worker, holds the valuable state.

## Build order

1. **Compile cache, shared scope first** — content-addressed key + R2 store + hit/miss
   in the compile lane. This alone removes most recompiles with **zero** new compute
   infra (misses still compile in-process for now). Biggest win per unit of work.
2. **`job` primitive + Fly worker** — baked compiler image, scale-to-zero, dispatched on
   a cache miss. Moves the heavy compile off the serving box.
3. **Private tenant scope + signing** — the security boundary, encrypted-at-rest.
4. **CI/CD jobs** — `flow`-over-`job` wired to `hook`/push/cron triggers; R2 artifact
   output; the two metered units in `Nexus.Config`.

Step 1 is independently valuable and ships first; the fleet (step 2) only pays off once
the cache is deciding what actually needs compiling.
